 Cleveland's Recent Chinese Acquisitions.\" <em>The Bulletin of the Cleveland Museum of Art </em>81, no. 8 (October 1994), p. 336, Table 1.&nbsp;<br>Ownership history based on copy of painting illustrated in album in Wang Shimin's collection, fig. 46, p. 339.</div>"],"date":null,"sortorder":4},{"description":"Geng Zhaozhong [1640–1686]","citations":[],"footnotes":["<div><!--block-->Provenance given by J. Keith Wilson in \"New Objects/New Insights: Cleveland's Recent Chinese Acquisitions.\" <em>The Bulletin of the Cleveland Museum of Art </em>81, no. 8 (October 1994), p. 336, Table 1.&nbsp;<br>Ownership history based on seals on painting, see fig. 45d-i, p. 337</div>"],"date":null,"sortorder":5},{"description":"Geng Jiazuo [1600s]","citations":[],"footnotes":["<div><!--block-->Provenance given by J. Keith Wilson in \"New Objects/New Insights: Cleveland's Recent Chinese Acquisitions.\" <em>The Bulletin of the Cleveland Museum of Art </em>81, no. 8 (October 1994), p. 336, Table 1.&nbsp;<br>Ownership history based on seal on painting, see fig. 45j, p. 337</div>"],"date":"1600s","sortorder":6},{"description":"An Qi 安岐 [1683–1717]","citations":[],"footnotes":["<div><!--block-->Provenance given by J. Keith Wilson in \"New Objects/New Insights: Cleveland's Recent Chinese Acquisitions.\" <em>The Bulletin of the Cleveland Museum of Art </em>81, no. 8 (October 1994), p. 336, Table 1.&nbsp;<br>Ownership history based on seal on painting, see fig. 45k, p. 337</div>"],"date":null,"sortorder":7},{"description":"\"Descendant of Wang Hongxu\"","citations":[],"footnotes":["<div><!--block-->Provenance given by J. Keith Wilson in \"New Objects/New Insights: Cleveland's Recent Chinese Acquisitions.\" <em>The Bulletin of the Cleveland Museum of Art </em>81, no. 8 (October 1994), p. 336, Table 1.&nbsp;<br>Ownership history based on seal on painting, see fig. 45L, p. 337</div>"],"date":"1700s?","sortorder":8},{"description":"Wang Baotian [1700s?–1800s?]","citations":[],"footnotes":["<div><!--block-->Provenance given by J. Keith Wilson in \"New Objects/New Insights: Cleveland's Recent Chinese Acquisitions.\" <em>The Bulletin of the Cleveland Museum of Art </em>81, no. 8 (October 1994), p. 336, Table 1.&nbsp;<br>Ownership history based on seal on painting, see fig. 45m, p. 337</div>"],"date":"1700s?–1800s?","sortorder":9},{"description":"Pei JIngfu [1855–1926]","citations":[],"footnotes":["<div><!--block-->Provenance given by J. Keith Wilson in \"New Objects/New Insights: Cleveland's Recent Chinese Acquisitions.\" <em>The Bulletin of the Cleveland Museum of Art </em>81, no. 8 (October 1994), p. 336, Table 1, and p. 341, footnote 13.&nbsp;<br>Ownership history based on record in catalogue.</div>"],"date":null,"sortorder":10},{"description":"(James J. Freeman, sold to the Cleveland Museum of Art)","citations":[],"footnotes":[],"date":"?–1992","sortorder":11},{"description":"The Cleveland Museum of Art, Cleveland, OH","citations":[],"footnotes":[],"date":"1992–","sortorder":12}],"find_spot":null,"related_works":[],"former_accession_numbers":[],"did_you_know":"The wet hills and moist, rich atmosphere evoke the southern Chinese summer, characterized by monsoon rains and lush growth.","early_education_description":null,"artlens_description":null,"description":"This monumental landscape is drawn in softly rounded strokes with darker lines frequently applied on top of lighter ones. This technique recalls one of the suggestions included in a collection of the artist's writings known as Secrets of Landscape Painting: The most difficult thing in painting is using the ink. The artist begins by using dilute ink and builds it up to the point where it begins to look right, then uses dense, black ink, applied fairly dry, and washes of deeper-toned ink. This technique distinguishes the fields from the paths, the far from the near. Huang Gongwang may have learned this technique from observing nature through the eyes of an earlier master, Dong Yuan, a painter whose style he shared.","external_resources":{"wikidata":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q60476782"],"internet_archive":["https://archive.org/details/clevelandart-1992.1-summer-mountains-aft"]},"citations":[{"citation":"Turner, Evan H. \"The Year in Review for 1992.\" <em>The Bulletin of the Cleveland Museum of Art</em> 80, no. 2 (February 1993): 38–79.","page_number":"Reproduced: p. 38; Mentioned: pp. 42–43, 77","url":"https://www.jstor.org/stable/25161388"},{"citation":"\"1993 Annual Report.\" <em>The Bulletin of the Cleveland Museum of Art</em> 81, no. 6 (July 1994): 143–218.","page_number":"Mentioned: p. 155","url":"https://www.jstor.org/stable/25161457"},{"citation":"Wilson, J. Keith, and Anne E. Wardwell. \"New Objects/New Insights: Cleveland's Recent Chinese Acquisitions.\" <em>The Bulletin of the Cleveland Museum of Art </em>81, no. 8 (October 1994): 270–347.","page_number":"Reproduced: p. 334, 336–337; Mentioned: pp. 333–341, 347","url":"https://www.jstor.org/stable/25161465"},{"citation":"Vanderstappen, Harrie A. \"<em>Summer Mountains after Dong Yuan</em> and Hong Gongwang.\" <em>Monumenta Serica</em>. <em>Journal of Oriental Studies</em>, no. 42 (December 1994), pp. 309–328.","page_number":"","url":"http://www.jstor.org/stable/40727021"},{"citation":"Cunningham, Michael R., Stanislaw J. Czuma, Anne E. Wardwell, and J. Keith Wilson. <em>Masterworks of Asian Art</em>. Cleveland, OH: The Cleveland Museum of Art, 1998.","page_number":"Mentioned and Reproduced: pp. 72–73","url":""},{"citation":"Chuanxi, Chen. “Huang Gongwang, the Master of the Yuan Painting Reform.” <em>Yi shu jia</em>, i. 613 (June 2026): 300–333.","page_number":"Mentioned and Reproduced: p. 305","url":""}],"url":"https://clevelandart.org/art/1992.1","images":{"annotation":null,"web":{"url":"https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/1992.1/1992.1_web.jpg","width":"388","height":"893","filesize":"216468","filename":"1992.1_web.jpg"},"print":{"url":"https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/1992.1/1992.1_pri